  "executive_briefing": "The landscape of global tax and trade policy in late 2026 is defined by the crystallization of landmark structural frameworks. The OECD Pillar Two global minimum tax is fully transitioning from policy design to enforcement across more than 140 jurisdictions, compelling multinational enterprises to overhaul their compliance structures. Concurrently, statutory corporate tax adjustments remain a priority, highlighted by the release of the 2026 OECD Corporate Tax Statistics and targeted regional relief, such as Singapore's newly defined corporate tax rebates for the 2026 Year of Assessment. In digital tax compliance, tax administrations are stepping up continuous transaction controls, with Singapore actively executing its phased Peppol-based InvoiceNow mandates for voluntary GST registrants ahead of eventual universal compliance. Meanwhile, trade policy faces intense friction as unilateral tariff strategies trigger robust legal pushbacks; notable battles include the U.S. Court of International Trade declaring emergency-power tariffs unlawful, while upcoming Supreme Court reviews and USMCA trilateral assessments keep supply chains in a state of high uncertainty.",
